%U A277187 149,151,157,163,167,169,173,179,181,191,193,197,199,211,223,227,229,233,239,241,251,257,263,269,271,277,281,283,289,293
%N A277187 Numbers k such that A001158(k) == 1 (mod k).
%C A277187 A087797 is a proper subsequence: sigma_3(p^k) == 1 (mod p^k) for k <= 3. Apart from 1, the terms that are not primes, squares of primes or cubes of primes are 36 = 2^2*3^2 and 3469595 = 5*67*10357; there are no others below 10^7. - _Victor X. Ocampo_, Aug 04 2026
%C A277187 Another example (not necessarily the next one) is 5 * 75013 * 303871 = 113971376615. - _Robert Israel_, Aug 06 2026
%H A277187 Andrew Howroyd, <a href="/A277187/b277187.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A277187 a(2) = 2 because sigma_3(2) = 1^3 + 2^3 = 9 and 9 == 1 (mod 2);
%e A277187 a(3) = 3 because sigma_3(3) = 1^3 + 3^3 = 28 and 28 == 1 (mod 3);
%e A277187 a(4) = 4 because sigma_3(4) = 1^3 + 2^3 + 4^3 = 73 and 73 == 1 (mod 4), etc.
%t A277187 Select[Range[300], Mod[DivisorSigma[3, #1] - 1, #1] == 0 & ]
%o A277187 (PARI) isok(k) = Mod(sigma(k, 3), k) == 1; \\ _Michel Marcus_, Aug 06 2026
